From c8ca0535bf3d712bd06dcfd065171b666eb55e78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: evpeng <ericvpeng@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:50:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a B-bit RaBitQ code in RAM and prune with its bound
 before reading raw vectors

store=rawmemex (STORE_ENV=rawmemex B_ENV=B): the SSD records are the raw
vectors, as with store=raw, and the extra-precision code (B-1 bits per
dimension, with its two factors) is kept in RAM next to the 1-bit code.
The scan refines every candidate that passes the 1-bit bound with the
B-bit estimate (split_distance_boosting) and prunes it when the B-bit
lower bound, est_B - f_error*g_error / 2^(B-1) (the same error term
split_single_fulldist uses, f_error*g_error being est_1 - low_1), exceeds
the current k-th distance; survivors carry the tighter bound into page
scheduling and are re-ranked exactly. Requires MEMDIM = padded_dim.

Validation on a 1M DEEP slice (C 977, 16 threads, nprobe 4-256), recall
against raw at the same nprobe: B=2 within 0.02 points everywhere, with
2.0-4.1x fewer pages and 1.8-2.3x the q/s; B=3 within 0.06 points, 2.7-7x
fewer pages; B=9 loses up to 1 point at low nprobe, so the 2^-(B-1)
scaling is optimistic at high B. RAM: 54 B/vector at B=2 vs 31 B for raw.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PHh2So74gbsJt7baRykiVp
